1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,615
Here's an experiment you
can do with your lunch.

2
00:00:02,615 --> 00:00:03,785
Take a plain cracker,

3
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:05,405
with no added sugar.

4
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:07,845
Chew it, and keep
chewing. Don't swallow.

5
00:00:07,845 --> 00:00:09,340
Give it a minute or so,

6
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,610
and it starts to
taste a little sweet.

7
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:13,175
But you didn't add any sugar.

8
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:15,490
So where's that
sweetness coming from?

9
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:17,590
A molecule in your saliva.

10
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:21,200
In a minute, it breaks links that
would otherwise last around a million

11
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:22,200
years.

12
00:00:24,682 --> 00:00:26,792
Crackers are packed with starch.

13
00:00:26,792 --> 00:00:29,967
Zoom right in, and starch
turns out to be a chain.

14
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:31,432
Hundreds, even thousands,

15
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:32,992
of glucose molecules,

16
00:00:32,992 --> 00:00:34,632
linked end to end.

17
00:00:34,632 --> 00:00:35,902
Glucose is a sugar,

18
00:00:35,902 --> 00:00:37,707
but locked up in a chain like this,

19
00:00:37,707 --> 00:00:39,257
it doesn't taste sweet.

20
00:00:39,257 --> 00:00:41,057
And those links are tough.

21
00:00:41,057 --> 00:00:43,427
Leave one in water
at room temperature,

22
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:45,965
and on average it would
last millions of years.

23
00:00:45,965 --> 00:00:47,325
Enter amylase:

24
00:00:47,325 --> 00:00:50,020
an enzyme made in
your salivary glands.

25
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:51,680
It's a carbohydrase:

26
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,920
it snips starch into smaller sugars,

27
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:57,785
like maltose, which is just two
glucose units joined together.

28
00:00:57,785 --> 00:00:59,945
And maltose does taste sweet.

29
00:00:59,945 --> 00:01:01,545
That's what an enzyme is:

30
00:01:01,545 --> 00:01:03,710
a biological catalyst.

31
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,340
It speeds up a reaction
without being used up,

32
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:08,405
and it comes out unchanged.

33
00:01:08,405 --> 00:01:10,875
Nearly every reaction
in your body has one,

34
00:01:10,875 --> 00:01:13,620
whether it's building things
up or breaking them down.

35
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:18,280
Without enzymes, your metabolism would
be far too slow to keep you alive.

36
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,710
Nearly all enzymes are proteins:

37
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:22,955
long chains of amino acids,

38
00:01:22,955 --> 00:01:24,960
folded into a precise shape.

39
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:26,828
And that shape is everything.

40
00:01:26,828 --> 00:01:30,178
Every enzyme has a small
pocket called the active site.

41
00:01:30,178 --> 00:01:31,597
The molecule it works on,

42
00:01:31,597 --> 00:01:33,767
the substrate, fits inside,

43
00:01:33,767 --> 00:01:36,517
forming an enzyme–substrate complex.

44
00:01:36,517 --> 00:01:39,737
Held there, a bond becomes
much easier to break.

45
00:01:39,737 --> 00:01:41,728
Snap. The products leave,

46
00:01:41,728 --> 00:01:44,453
and the active site is
free for the next one.

47
00:01:44,453 --> 00:01:48,517
The active site is complementary to
the substrate: not the same shape,

48
00:01:48,517 --> 00:01:49,918
but a perfect fit.

49
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:51,692
The enzyme is the lock.

50
00:01:51,692 --> 00:01:53,312
The substrate is the key.

51
00:01:53,312 --> 00:01:55,703
That's why enzymes are specific.

52
00:01:55,703 --> 00:01:58,368
Each one only fits its own substrate.

53
00:01:58,368 --> 00:02:01,257
Amylase can't even digest paper.

54
00:02:01,257 --> 00:02:02,737
It's mostly cellulose,

55
00:02:02,737 --> 00:02:04,263
another chain of glucose,

56
00:02:04,263 --> 00:02:06,238
but its links are joined differently,

57
00:02:06,238 --> 00:02:07,793
so it just doesn't fit.

58
00:02:07,793 --> 00:02:09,542
Lock and key is a brilliant model,

59
00:02:09,542 --> 00:02:11,278
but it's a simplification.

60
00:02:11,278 --> 00:02:13,468
Real enzymes aren't rigid.

61
00:02:13,468 --> 00:02:14,768
As the substrate binds,

62
00:02:14,768 --> 00:02:17,208
the active site moulds
itself around it.

63
00:02:17,208 --> 00:02:20,173
That more accurate picture
is called induced fit.

64
00:02:20,173 --> 00:02:23,007
For GCSE and IGCSE,

65
00:02:23,007 --> 00:02:25,013
lock and key is all you need.

66
00:02:25,013 --> 00:02:28,643
Induced fit is an A-level
idea. Just remember:

67
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:31,405
it's a model, not a photograph.

68
00:02:31,405 --> 00:02:33,300
Which brings us to myth number one:

69
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:36,020
that enzymes get used up. They don't.

70
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:37,400
After each reaction,

71
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:41,120
the enzyme is unchanged,
ready to go again, and again.

72
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:43,520
And some are astonishingly fast.

73
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,485
Catalase is found in
almost all your cells.

74
00:02:46,485 --> 00:02:50,095
One molecule of it can break down
hundreds of thousands of molecules of

75
00:02:50,095 --> 00:02:53,015
hydrogen peroxide. Every second.

76
00:02:53,015 --> 00:02:55,455
Your cells do replace old enzymes,

77
00:02:55,455 --> 00:02:59,451
eventually. But the reaction
itself never uses them up.

78
00:02:59,451 --> 00:03:00,906
Now, temperature.

79
00:03:00,906 --> 00:03:03,446
Molecules are always jiggling around.

80
00:03:03,446 --> 00:03:07,486
Heat them up, and they gain
kinetic energy. They move faster.

81
00:03:07,486 --> 00:03:10,616
Enzymes and substrates bump
into each other more often,

82
00:03:10,616 --> 00:03:12,101
and with more energy.

83
00:03:12,101 --> 00:03:14,166
So more collisions are successful,

84
00:03:14,166 --> 00:03:16,931
with the substrate landing
in the active site.

85
00:03:16,931 --> 00:03:19,436
More enzyme–substrate complexes form,

86
00:03:19,436 --> 00:03:20,916
and the rate goes up.

87
00:03:20,916 --> 00:03:23,781
Up to a point, warmer means faster.

88
00:03:23,781 --> 00:03:26,961
The temperature where the rate
peaks is called the optimum.

89
00:03:26,961 --> 00:03:28,711
For many enzymes in your body,

90
00:03:28,711 --> 00:03:32,145
it's around 37 degrees:
body temperature.

91
00:03:32,145 --> 00:03:35,100
So what if we keep turning
up the heat? Fifty degrees.

92
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,805
Sixty. Seventy.

93
00:03:36,805 --> 00:03:38,900
Does it just keep getting faster?

94
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:42,390
Pause the video, and
make your prediction.

95
00:03:45,005 --> 00:03:46,785
Here's what actually happens.

96
00:03:46,785 --> 00:03:49,170
The rate crashes, and
it doesn't recover.

97
00:03:49,170 --> 00:03:52,805
An enzyme's shape is held
together by lots of weak bonds.

98
00:03:52,805 --> 00:03:57,235
Too much heat, and it vibrates so
violently that those bonds break.

99
00:03:57,235 --> 00:03:58,615
The chain unravels,

100
00:03:58,615 --> 00:04:00,685
the active site loses its shape,

101
00:04:00,685 --> 00:04:02,970
and the substrate no longer fits.

102
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:05,200
The enzyme has been denatured.

103
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:09,630
It's the same kind of change that
turns egg white solid in a hot pan.

104
00:04:09,630 --> 00:04:11,150
You can't uncook an egg,

105
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:14,035
and cooling usually won't
fix the enzyme either.

106
00:04:14,035 --> 00:04:15,525
Cold is different.

107
00:04:15,525 --> 00:04:17,695
Molecules have less kinetic energy,

108
00:04:17,695 --> 00:04:19,530
so there are fewer collisions.

109
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:21,705
But the enzyme isn't denatured.

110
00:04:21,705 --> 00:04:23,800
Warm it up, and it recovers.

111
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:25,760
And that's myth number two:

112
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,700
that heat "kills" enzymes.

113
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,540
Enzymes were never alive.

114
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,245
An enzyme is a molecule,

115
00:04:31,245 --> 00:04:33,655
a very clever one, but it can't die.

116
00:04:33,655 --> 00:04:35,060
It's denatured.

117
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:36,945
Its active site changes shape,

118
00:04:36,945 --> 00:04:38,820
so the substrate no longer fits,

119
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:41,905
and no enzyme–substrate
complex can form.

120
00:04:41,905 --> 00:04:44,916
In an exam, that's the
answer that gets the marks.

121
00:04:44,916 --> 00:04:48,306
pH, how acidic or
alkaline something is,

122
00:04:48,306 --> 00:04:50,671
can denature enzymes too.

123
00:04:50,671 --> 00:04:52,101
Too far either way,

124
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:55,561
and the bonds holding the active
site in shape are disrupted.

125
00:04:55,561 --> 00:04:58,436
So every enzyme has an optimum pH.

126
00:04:58,436 --> 00:04:59,681
Amylase, in your mouth,

127
00:04:59,681 --> 00:05:01,361
works best close to neutral,

128
00:05:01,361 --> 00:05:03,196
around pH 7.

129
00:05:03,196 --> 00:05:05,201
Your stomach is strongly acidic,

130
00:05:05,201 --> 00:05:07,371
often around pH 2.

131
00:05:07,371 --> 00:05:10,661
And pepsin, a protease that
digests protein there,

132
00:05:10,661 --> 00:05:13,681
works best right around
that. Different enzymes,

133
00:05:13,681 --> 00:05:15,578
tuned to different places.

134
00:05:15,578 --> 00:05:17,308
So, back to that cracker.

135
00:05:17,308 --> 00:05:19,968
As you chew, amylase
meets the starch.

136
00:05:19,968 --> 00:05:21,903
The chains slot into its active site,

137
00:05:21,903 --> 00:05:23,443
and it snips them into maltose,

138
00:05:23,443 --> 00:05:25,588
over and over, without being used up.

139
00:05:25,588 --> 00:05:27,603
Your mouth is about 37 degrees,

140
00:05:27,603 --> 00:05:30,073
and close to neutral. Just right.

141
00:05:30,073 --> 00:05:33,023
Links that would last a million
years or more on their own?

142
00:05:33,023 --> 00:05:34,543
Broken while you chew.

143
00:05:34,543 --> 00:05:36,048
That sweet taste is you,

144
00:05:36,048 --> 00:05:38,108
tasting enzymes at work.

145
00:05:38,108 --> 00:05:39,803
It all comes down to shape.

146
00:05:39,803 --> 00:05:42,093
And that's how enzymes actually work.
